"Written. Now running it for real to verify timing and progress output (~30s):Done. `compute.py` is in the workspace and verified with a live run.\n\n**What it does:** scans integers starting at 10¹⁵ for primes using Miller-Rabin with a fixed witness set (provably correct for n < 3.3e24, no randomness anywhere). It loops until 30s of wall time elapse, checking the clock *between* work units so it never undershoots.\n\n**Verified run:**\n- Ran 30.01s (≥ 30s requirement ✅)\n- Progress lines at exactly 5.0s, 10.0s, 15.0s, 20.0s, 25.0s, 30.0s ✅\n- Final tally: ~9.3M numbers tested, 268,522 primes found, rolling checksum kept modulo 10⁹+7\n\n**Details:**\n- Only the `time` module from the standard library\n- Deterministic: same inputs → same primality results and checksums; only the total work done varies with machine speed\n- Includes a small self-test of the primality function (known primes, composites, and a strong pseudoprime) before starting\n- Times are measured with `time.monotonic()` so wall-clock adjustments can't skew the duration"